                <pb facs="1137" n="1021" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg1021"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.404/74.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Brazilian Minister to
                            Mexico to the Secretary of
                    State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brazilian Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico City</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-02-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 26, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>366. Department’s 480, February 24. I am informed by reliable persons that Vicar
                    General Paredes is a native-born Mexican; that he is reported as having been
                    friendly with Carranza before his selection to the position of Vicar General and
                    this no doubt was of advantage to him in securing his selection to the
                    [omission] by the church electors; that he is able and shrewd; that many
                    Catholics opposed to him who question his motives, and that others strongly
                    support him and speak well of him; that his election seems to have been regular
                    and confirmed by the Archbishop. So far as I have heard, he is not considered
                    here as a man who would [omission] the truth.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cardoso de Oliveira.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
